# Partition layout for the Radxa ZERO 3E.
#
# Based on wic/generic-gptdisk.wks.in (this layer's default), with two
# kinds of change on top of the original single-rootfs layout:
#
# - The U-Boot proper partition moves from offset 2M to 8M: U-Boot's
#   SPL (see u-boot-radxa-zero3e_2024.07.bb) loads u-boot.itb from a
#   *fixed raw sector* (not a GPT lookup by partition name), computed
#   from CONFIG_SPL_PAD_TO=0x7f8000 in the board defconfig - i.e.
#   sector 64 (where loader1/idbloader starts) + 0x7f8000/512 sectors =
#   sector 16384 = offset 8MiB. This is exactly the offset the official
#   upstream meta-rockchip layer's own wic/rockchip.wks uses for the
#   same reason.
#
# - Symmetric A/B (RAUC): "boot"/"rootfs" became boot_a/boot_b (vfat,
#   kernel+dtb+shared extlinux.conf) and rootfs_a/rootfs_b (ext4), plus
#   a new persistent "data" partition outside the A/B scheme entirely.
#   See meta-toletus/recipes-core (rauc-data-sync, rauc-mark-good) and
#   meta-rockchip's own u-boot-radxa-zero3e_2024.07.bb (rauc-ab.cfg) for
#   the rest of this scheme - this file only owns the partition table.
#
#   Both boot_a/boot_b use the SAME --configfile= (radxa-zero3e-extlinux.conf,
#   or meta-toletus's own fork via its WKS_FILE override) - confirmed by
#   reading scripts/lib/wic/plugins/source/bootimg-partition.py that
#   "configfile = cr.ks.bootloader.configfile" comes from the wks file's
#   single global "bootloader --configfile=" directive, so there is no
#   way to give the two boot_* partitions different extlinux.conf
#   *content* in one wks file. That's why extlinux.conf's root= is
#   "${rauc_root}", a U-Boot env var the rauc-ab.cfg boot command sets
#   to the winning slot's PARTUUID= before calling sysboot, rather than
#   a literal PARTUUID baked into two different files.
#
#   Sizes: rootfs_a/rootfs_b are fixed (not auto-sized like the old
#   single rootfs partition was) - both slots must be identical capacity.
#   3072M each, sized around the current ~350M build plus the ~2500M
#   growth headroom expected for this board's sysroot, confirmed against
#   its real 16GB SD card (see meta-toletus's planning notes - total
#   partitioned space here is ~10.1GB, leaving >4.5GB unallocated
#   deliberately). "data" is 4096M: current models/media is ~100M, but
#   it also needs room to stage a full downloaded RAUC bundle
#   (boot+rootfs image, worst case ~3GB) before install.
#
#   "data" also needs --mkfs-extraopts disabling metadata_csum/64bit:
#   confirmed on real hardware (2026-10-09) that this U-Boot's EXT4
#   write support (CONFIG_ENV_IS_IN_EXT4, meta-rockchip's
#   u-boot-radxa-zero3e rauc-ab.cfg - U-Boot needs read+write access to
#   this exact partition for its own persistent env) refuses to write
#   at all if metadata_csum is present ("Saving Environment to EXT4...
#   Unsupported feature metadata_csum found, not writing"), a known
#   U-Boot ext4-write limitation. e2fsprogs enables it by default on a
#   plain mkfs.ext4 with no options, which is exactly what this
#   partition got before this line existed (it has no --mkfs-extraopts
#   at all, unlike rootfs_a/rootfs_b's RK_ROOTFS_EXTRAOPTS). 64bit
#   disabled defensively alongside it - not itself confirmed as a
#   problem here, but a very commonly co-occurring U-Boot/ext4-write
#   incompatibility in the same class, cheap to rule out up front rather
#   than discover on a second round-trip. rootfs_a/rootfs_b don't need
#   this: U-Boot never writes to or reads files from those directly (it
#   only needs raw root= mounting, which Linux's own ext4 driver - no
#   such limitation - handles once it takes over).
#
# RK_ROOTDEV_UUID_A/RK_ROOTDEV_UUID_B (classes/rockchip-image.bbclass)
# MUST stay in sync with the literal rauc_uuid_a/rauc_uuid_b values
# compiled into u-boot-radxa-zero3e's rauc-ab.cfg - see that file's own
# comment.
#
# The "boot" partition is a real vfat filesystem with a
# /extlinux/extlinux.conf (see wic/radxa-zero3e-extlinux.conf, wired up
# below via "bootloader --configfile="), not a raw copy of the
# Rockchip-style boot.img - mainline U-Boot's autoboot
# (distro_bootcmd/bootstd, what u-boot-radxa-zero3e uses by default)
# only knows how to find a kernel via /extlinux/extlinux.conf on an
# actual filesystem partition; it has no way to load a raw Android-style
# boot.img. RAUC's own CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND override (rauc-ab.cfg) calls
# "sysboot" directly against whichever boot_a/boot_b partition wins,
# rather than relying on distro_bootcmd's own device/partition scanning.
#
# A *custom* extlinux.conf is used instead of letting the
# "bootimg-partition" source plugin auto-generate one (its default
# "fdtdir /" line): U-Boot's compiled-in "fdtfile" env value is
# "rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3e.dtb" (see
# board/radxa/zero3-rk3566/zero3-rk3566.c upstream), and it's
# concatenated as-is onto fdtdir - i.e. it looks for
# "/rockchip/rk3566-radxa-zero-3e.dtb", not where IMAGE_BOOT_FILES
# actually placed the dtb ("/rk3566-radxa-zero-3e.dtb", flat). When
# that lookup fails, extlinux silently falls back to whatever
# devicetree U-Boot itself already had loaded - which is U-Boot's OWN
# (mainline, minimal) devicetree, not the kernel's vendor one. Booting
# the 6.1 vendor kernel with U-Boot's devicetree instead of its own
# causes a silent hang right after "Starting kernel ..." (no crash
# message, no console output at all - confirmed on real hardware).
# An explicit "fdt" line sidesteps fdtfile/fdtdir matching entirely.
#
# --active is required on both boot_a/boot_b, not cosmetic: it sets the
# GPT "legacy_boot" attribute bit (wic runs `parted set <n> legacy_boot
# on` for GPT when this flag is given). U-Boot's bootdev_find_in_blk()
# (boot/bootdev-uclass.c) calls part_get_bootable() on the whole disk
# first; if it finds *no* partition with that bit set, it falls back to
# only ever scanning partition 1 and rejects every other partition with
# -EINVAL ("No partition found") *before* even checking for a
# filesystem - which is exactly what happened to partitions 2 (boot)
# and 3 (rootfs) without this flag on the original single-slot layout,
# confirmed via `bootflow scan -lae` on the actual board. Our own
# CONFIG_BOOTCOMMAND override bypasses that scan entirely, so this is
# belt-and-suspenders now rather than a hard functional requirement -
# kept on both partitions anyway since there's no downside.
#
# boot_a/boot_b/data have NO mountpoint argument at all (unlike the old
# single "boot" partition, which used "/boot") - confirmed necessary on
# real hardware (2026-10-09), and confirmed the mechanism by reading
# wic itself, not guessed:
#   - wic's own update_fstab() (scripts/lib/wic/plugins/imager/
#     direct.py) runs ONCE per image, unconditionally unless wic's own
#     *global* --no-fstab-update CLI option is set (nothing in this
#     project's build passes that) - it writes one /etc/fstab line for
#     every partition whose mountpoint is set and isn't "/". It does
#     NOT consult the per-"part"-line --no-fstab-update keyword at all
#     (that one only controls whether an already-rootfs-sourced
#     partition gets the updated fstab baked into its OWN filesystem
#     via debugfs in prepare_rootfs() - a different step entirely, and
#     one boot_a/boot_b/data never go through anyway since none of them
#     use --source rootfs). A first attempt at this fix added that
#     per-partition flag to all three and changed nothing on real
#     hardware - confirming it doesn't gate this.
#   - With mountpoint "/boot" on both boot_a and boot_b, update_fstab()
#     wrote TWO conflicting /boot lines, which systemd-fstab-generator
#     then failed to turn into two different "boot.mount" units
#     ("Duplicate entry in /etc/fstab?") - the surviving one hung for
#     the full 90s mount timeout and dropped to emergency mode.
#   - bootimg-partition.py's own plugin code never references
#     part.mountpoint at all (confirmed by reading it) - the "/boot"
#     string wasn't doing anything useful for either partition in the
#     first place, just triggering this bug.
#   - Neither boot_a nor boot_b should ever be Linux-mounted at all in
#     normal operation anyway - only U-Boot touches them directly, and
#     RAUC writes updates to the inactive one by device path
#     (system.conf), not via fstab.
#   - Same reasoning for "data" (dropped "/data" as its mountpoint
#     too): a wic-auto-generated fstab entry for it would generate its
#     own "data.mount" unit that could shadow the hand-written one this
#     project ships (recipes-toletus/rauc-data-sync, meta-toletus) with
#     none of that unit's actual ordering/options - better to have
#     exactly one source of truth for that mount, and
#     prepare_empty_partition_ext4() (what actually builds this
#     partition, since it has no --source) doesn't use mountpoint
#     either, only --label.
#   - rootfs_a/rootfs_b keep "/" - update_fstab() already skips
#     mountpoint "/" unconditionally, so there was never a bug there.
#
# 0~32K: GPT
bootloader --ptable gpt --configfile=radxa-zero3e-extlinux.conf
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=idblock.img" --align 32 --no-table
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=uboot.img" --part-name uboot --offset 8M
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=trust.img" --part-name trust --align 4
part --source bootimg-partition --sourceparams="loader=u-boot" --part-name boot_a --label boot_a --fstype=vfat --align 4 --fixed-size 64M --active --use-uuid
part --source bootimg-partition --sourceparams="loader=u-boot" --part-name boot_b --label boot_b --fstype=vfat --align 4 --fixed-size 64M --active --use-uuid
part / --source rootfs --fstype ${RK_ROOTFS_TYPE} --part-name rootfs_a --label rootfs_a --uuid ${RK_ROOTDEV_UUID_A} --mkfs-extraopts "${RK_ROOTFS_EXTRAOPTS}" --align 8192 --fixed-size 3072M
part / --source rootfs --fstype ${RK_ROOTFS_TYPE} --part-name rootfs_b --label rootfs_b --uuid ${RK_ROOTDEV_UUID_B} --mkfs-extraopts "${RK_ROOTFS_EXTRAOPTS}" --align 8192 --fixed-size 3072M
part --fstype ext4 --part-name data --label data --align 8192 --fixed-size 4096M --mkfs-extraopts "-O ^metadata_csum,^64bit"
